From: Joonhoe Kim <26rote@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] Start sensors that were claimed before they were opened

With the SSC backend, find_sensors() runs synchronous QMI calls that
iterate the main context, so a ClaimAccelerometer() sent right after the
name is acquired (GNOME does this at login) is handled while the device
is still being opened. The claim then only registers the client, and
because later claims are not the first one, polling never starts:
GNOME's automatic rotation stays at "undefined". Start every claimed
sensor once the initial discovery is done, as sensor_changes() already
does for hotplugged sensors.

Applies to iio-sensor-proxy 3.9 (0085ddf8).
---
diff --git a/src/iio-sensor-proxy.c b/src/iio-sensor-proxy.c
index 9c7e8cd..9dda57b 100644
--- a/src/iio-sensor-proxy.c
+++ b/src/iio-sensor-proxy.c
@@ -781,6 +781,22 @@ name_acquired_handler (GDBusConnection *connection,
 		DEVICE_FOR_TYPE(i) = sensor_device;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Clients may have claimed a sensor while find_sensors() was still
+	 * discovering it: the SSC drivers run synchronous QMI calls that
+	 * iterate the main context, so Claim*() is dispatched before the
+	 * device exists and only registers the client. Start the claimed
+	 * sensors now, as sensor_changes() does for hotplugged ones.
+	 */
+	for (i = 0; i < NUM_SENSOR_TYPES; i++) {
+		if (!DEVICE_FOR_TYPE(i) || g_hash_table_size (data->clients[i]) == 0)
+			continue;
+		g_debug ("%s already claimed before it was opened, starting it",
+			 driver_type_to_str (i));
+		data->sensor_startup_dbus_event_delayed[i] = TRUE;
+		driver_set_polling (DEVICE_FOR_TYPE(i), TRUE);
+	}
+
 	if (!any_sensors_left (data))
 		goto bail;
 
